.TH ALESS 1 "2026-11-06" "aless 0.7.1" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
aless \- JSON, YAML, TOML, CSV, XML, INI, Markdown, and more as one tree, in a
jless\-style terminal viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
aless [OPTIONS] [FILE]...
.br
aless [OPTIONS] DIRECTORY
.br
COMMAND | aless [OPTIONS]
.PP
\-h prints a summary of the options. \-\-help prints this whole reference.
.SH DESCRIPTION
aless reads JSON, JSON Lines, JSON5, JSONC, jsonic, YAML, TOML, INI, CSV, TSV,
XML, ZON, Markdown, RSS or Atom feeds, CSS, Protocol Buffers .proto files, PGN
chess games, C source, expressions, semantic versions, and any line\-oriented
format an ABNF grammar describes. It also reads grammars written in ABNF, EBNF,
or GBNF. It shows each input as one tree, in a jless\-style viewer in the
terminal. The viewer has tabs, search, a source view, and a watch mode. The
watch mode reloads a changed file and keeps your place.
.SS "The viewer"
aless is a viewer for the terminal. Each FILE opens in a tab of its own, as a
tree: you can fold the tree, search it, and copy from it. A directory opens in
the explorer. With no FILE, aless reads standard input when standard input is
not a terminal. Otherwise aless explores the current directory. aless watches
every file: when a file changes on disk, aless reads it again and keeps your
place. A file that does not parse opens as the parser\(aqs report. The report
gives the line and column where the parse stopped.
.PP
aless refuses to start without a terminal to draw on and read keys from. It
refuses when standard output is a pipe or a file, when there is no terminal to
open, or when TERM is dumb. It refuses before reading any input. It writes one
line on standard error and exits with the status 2. Only \-h, \-\-help, \-V,
\-\-generate, and \-\-print\-json write to standard output. \-\-print\-json is
the one option that reads an input without a terminal: it prints the input\(aqs
tree as JSON, and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Input options"
.TP 7
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kind\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Read every input as FORMAT, whatever its extension says. FORMAT is a
format\(aqs name (json, jsonl, jsonic, jsonc, json5, yaml, toml, ini, csv, tsv,
xml, zon, markdown, feed, css, proto, pgn, c, abnf, ebnf, gbnf, expr, semver,
or text), one of its extensions (yml, md, ndjson), or a \-\-grammar NAME.
Standard input is read as JSON unless this option says otherwise. \-\-format is
another name for this option.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\fR \fINAME=FILE\fR
Read every file whose extension or whole name is NAME (x.hosts, /etc/hosts)
with the ABNF grammar in FILE. The match ignores case. NAME is then a format:
\-k and :format take it, and the status bar shows it. NAME,NAME2=FILE gives the
format two names. You can give the option more than once. aless reads and
compiles FILE before it reads any input, so a FILE that does not compile is a
usage error. The read is held to \-\-max\-size, and the compile to \-\-timeout.
For more information, see CUSTOM GRAMMARS.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\-expr\fR \fINAME=ABNF\fR
Do what \-\-grammar does, with the grammar\(aqs text on the command line in
place of FILE. The text is everything after the first =.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-max\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR
Refuse an input larger than SIZE, a \-\-grammar file included. The default is
64M, and 0 is no limit. SIZE is bytes, or a whole number with K, M, or G,
counted in 1024s. A file over the limit opens in a tab that says so and names
the size that would read it. A grammar file over the limit is a usage error. A
parse needs about 40 bytes of memory for each byte of input.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a parse, or a \-\-grammar compile, that runs longer than SECONDS. SECONDS
is a number of seconds (2.5, 90s) or of minutes (2m). The default is none, and
0 is no limit. An input whose parse is stopped opens in a tab that says how far
the parse got. A compile that is stopped is a usage error. A parse runs at
about 1 MB a second. For more information, see LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS.
.SS "Viewer options"
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR
Do not reload a file when it changes on disk. Inside aless, W or :watch on
starts watching a tab.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-watch\fR
Reload a file when it changes, and keep your place in it. Reloading is the
default.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-changes\fR
Do not mark what each reload changed. aless keeps the versions all the same,
and [ and ] show them. Inside aless, :set changes turns the marks on.
.TP 7
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Start in data mode or in line mode. The default is data mode. Data mode shows
the tree without its punctuation. Line mode shows the tree as JSON prints it,
with the punctuation that data mode leaves out. Inside aless, m switches
between them.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Open only the first N levels when a file opens. A container N levels below the
root starts folded, and so does everything in it. So \-\-depth 0 folds the
root. By default, every container is open. Inside aless, :depth N opens the
first N levels in the same way.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-indent\fR \fIN\fR
Indent the tree N spaces a level. The default is 2, and the limit is 16. Inside
aless, < and > change the indent.
.TP 7
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-line\-numbers\fR
Number the rows on screen. Each number is the count g or G needs to reach the
row.
.TP 7
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-numbers\fR
Do not number the rows. This option undoes an \-n before it.
.TP 7
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Number each row by its distance from the focused row. That distance is the
count j or k needs to reach the row. With \-n as well, the focused row shows
its own number.
.TP 7
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-no\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Do not number the rows by distance. This option undoes an \-r before it.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-scrolloff\fR \fIN\fR
Keep N rows between the focused row and the top or the bottom of the screen.
The default is 3.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-ascii\fR
Draw the fold markers as v and >, for a font without triangles. By default,
aless draws the markers as triangles.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-colour\fR
Draw without colour, as when NO_COLOR is set.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal, for its own selection. By default, aless takes
the wheel and the clicks of the mouse.
.SS "Other options"
.TP 7
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options (\-h), or this whole reference (\-\-help).
.TP 7
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version: aless 0.7.1.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-generate\fR \fIWHAT\fR
Print a file written from this reference. WHAT is man for the man page. The man
page is aless.1. WHAT is complete\-bash, complete\-zsh, complete\-fish, or
complete\-powershell for that shell\(aqs completions. For more information, see
FILES.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-print\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR
Print the tree of FILE as JSON on standard output, and exit. The viewer does
not start, so no terminal is needed. FILE is read as the viewer reads it: \-k,
\-\-grammar, \-\-grammar\-expr, \-\-max\-size, and \-\-timeout apply, and the
viewer\(aqs options make no difference. FILE \- is standard input, read as JSON
unless \-k says otherwise. The JSON is what :w writes: two spaces a level, the
keys in their order in the source. When FILE cannot be read or does not parse,
the report goes to standard error and the exit status is 1.
.SH "INPUT AND FORMATS"
FILE is a path, or \- for standard input. Options end at \-\-, so every
argument after \-\- is a FILE, even one that starts with \-. A long option\(aqs
value follows it, or is attached with = (\-\-kind=yaml).
.PP
A file\(aqs format comes from its extension, or from its whole name when that
name is a \-\-grammar NAME (/etc/hosts). \-k FORMAT overrides the format for
every input. Inside aless, :format FORMAT reads a tab again as FORMAT, and
:open PATH FORMAT opens a file as FORMAT. A file whose extension no format
claims is read as plain text: an array of its lines.
.PP
Keys keep their order in the source. A number keeps its spelling where JSON
spells it the same way. So 1.0, 1e21, and an integer beyond 2\(ha53 are shown,
searched, copied, and written by :w as the source writes them. Where JSON has
no syntax for a spelling, such as 0x1F, 1_000, or +1, aless reads the number as
the 64\-bit float that the spelling stands for. The source view (s) shows the
spelling.
.PP
These are the formats, each with the extensions that imply it:
