.SH "KEYS"
The keys are those of jless. Inside aless, F1 or :help lists every key and
command. These are the keys used most:
.TP 15
\fBj k\fR
Move down and up a row. A count before a key repeats it (3j).
.TP 15
\fBh l\fR
Fold and unfold, or go to the parent and the first child.
.TP 15
\fBJ K\fR
Go to the next and the previous sibling.
.TP 15
\fBg G\fR
Go to the first and the last row. Ng goes to row N.
.TP 15
\fBCtrl+D Ctrl+U\fR
Move half a page down and up.
.TP 15
\fBSpace\fR
Fold or unfold the focused container.
.TP 15
\fBc e\fR
Fold and unfold the focused node and its siblings.
.TP 15
\fB/ ?\fR
Search forward and backward. The keys n and N go to the next and the previous
match.
.TP 15
\fByy yp yq\fR
Copy the value, its path, or its path as jq writes it.
.TP 15
\fBm\fR
Switch between data mode and line mode.
.TP 15
\fBs\fR
Show the source view, at the focused node\(aqs line.
.TP 15
\fB!\fR
Show the parse error report full size.
.TP 15
\fBTab Shift+Tab\fR
Go to the next and the previous tab.
.TP 15
\fB:\fR
Start a command, such as :open PATH, :format FORMAT, :w FILE, or :help.
.TP 15
\fBq\fR
Close the tab. Closing the last tab quits. Ctrl+C quits.
.SH "CUSTOM GRAMMARS"
\-\-grammar NAME=FILE uses the ABNF grammar in FILE to read every file whose
extension or whole name is NAME. The match ignores case. The grammar is ABNF as
RFC 5234 defines it, and tabnas/abnf compiles it. \-k NAME reads any input with
the grammar, standard input included. Comments on a rule say what it builds: ;
@object a b makes an object of its parts a and b, and ; @array makes an array.
Without them, the value is the compiler\(aqs parse tree: a {"rule", "src",
"kids"} node for each rule.
.PP
A grammar reads its input as plain text. In the input, the characters { } [ ] :
and , are ordinary, # starts a comment that runs to the end of the line, and a
word is the token TX. NR brings back numbers, ST brings back quoted strings,
and VL brings back true, false, and null.
.PP
A grammar that does not compile is refused as a usage error, before any input
is read. So is a repetition count over 1,024, or repetitions that would have
the compiler write more than 1,024 rules. An input the grammar does not accept
opens as a parse error. The input\(aqs format is the grammar\(aqs name.
.PP
The source of aless holds grammars for /etc/hosts, crontabs, /etc/passwd,
/etc/group, /etc/fstab, /etc/resolv.conf, and KEY=value files. Each grammar
comes with a sample. The grammars, their samples, and a guide to writing a
grammar are in this directory:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
https://github.com/rjrodger/aless/tree/main/tests/fixtures/grammars
.fi
.RE
.SH "LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS"
aless reads an input whole, and parses it whole, before it shows anything. A
parse runs at about 1 MB a second. It needs about 40 bytes of memory for each
byte of input. The viewer waits for the parse, so aless says when it starts to
read a large file. A reload of a large watched file pauses the viewer until the
parse ends.
.PP
\-\-max\-size refuses an input larger than its limit before reading it. The
default limit is 64M. An input over the limit opens in a tab that says so and
names the size that would read it. \-\-timeout stops a parse that runs too
long. An input whose parse is stopped opens in a tab that says how far the
parse got.
.PP
A document nested too deep for aless opens as a parse error with the code
too_deep. Every parse stops at 3,000 open rules, about 1,000 levels. Most
grammars stop sooner. JSON, JSONL, jsonic, JSON5, YAML, TOML, INI, ZON, and
expressions stop at 127 levels. XML stops at 256, JSONC at 512, and CSS at 768
open rules. The braces of a .proto file can nest 100 deep. aless counts the
braces before it parses the file. The Markdown tree stops at 205 levels,
without an error.
.PP
C stops at an expression of about 127 operations, at 1,024 open rules, or at a
syntax tree 256 levels deep. Its tree repeats subtrees, so C also stops at a
tree with more than 256 values for each byte of the file, or with more than
2,048 bytes of names and strings for each byte of the file. Those limits are
between 1,048,576 and 33,554,432 values, and between 8 MB and 256 MB. A grammar
in ABNF, EBNF, or GBNF can nest its groups and elements 127 deep.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 7
\fB0\fR
You quit the viewer, or \-\-print\-json printed its file\(aqs tree.
.TP 7
\fB1\fR
The viewer failed while it ran: aless could not draw on the terminal or read
from it. Or \-\-print\-json could not read its file, or the file did not parse,
and the report is on standard error.
.TP 7
\fB2\fR
The viewer could not start, or \-\-print\-json could not. Either there was a
usage error, or there was no terminal to draw on and read keys from. A usage
error is an unknown option, a bad value, or a \-\-grammar that does not compile
or cannot be read.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP 10
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
set and not empty: draw without colour, as \-\-no\-color does
.TP 10
\fBTERM\fR
dumb: a terminal that cannot draw the viewer, so aless refuses to start, as it
does without a terminal
.SH "FILES"
A release archive carries the man page and the completions. \-\-generate prints
them as well:
.TP 18
\fBman/aless.1\fR
the man page (\-\-generate man)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.bash\fR
the completions for bash (\-\-generate complete\-bash)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless\fR
the completions for zsh (\-\-generate complete\-zsh)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.fish\fR
the completions for fish (\-\-generate complete\-fish)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless.ps1\fR
the completions for PowerShell (\-\-generate complete\-powershell)
.PP
A shell reads its completions from where it looks for them. A package puts them
there. A shell can read them from its startup file instead, each time it
starts:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-bash)"     # \(ti/.bashrc
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-zsh)"      # \(ti/.zshrc, after compinit
aless \-\-generate complete\-fish | source      # config.fish
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.nf
aless config.yaml                      one file, in a tab
aless deploy.yaml values.toml a.csv    several files, a tab each
curl \-s https://api.example/x | aless  standard input, read as JSON
aless \-k yaml settings.conf            a file whose extension misleads
aless \-\-grammar hosts=hosts.abnf /etc/hosts
                                       a format of your own
aless \-\-no\-watch big.json              no reload when the file changes
aless \-m line \-n x.json                line mode, the rows numbered
aless                                  explore the current directory
.fi
